.meraki-snapshots/

# crew-ignore-policy:list - this stanza is the AUTHORITY for the `.crew/` un-ignore
# list - it is what git actually obeys. `check_crew_ignore_policy` in
# scripts/check-marketplace.py reads the list from here and fails any file
# carrying the same marker that states a different set.
#
# Per-repo crew state. Machine-specific by nature: obsidian.vaultPath and
# boardDir are absolute paths that exist on one machine, and pm.authority is a
# trust decision its owner makes, not one a clone should inherit. Treat it like
# .vscode/settings.json. A fresh clone runs /crew:init to generate its own.
#
# `.crew/*` rather than `.crew/`: a trailing slash makes git refuse to descend
# into the directory at all, and an un-entered directory cannot have anything
# re-included from it. The glob lets the negation below work.
.crew/*

# ...except the code map, which is the one thing under .crew/ that is about the
# CODE rather than about this machine. It holds prose and `path:line` citations
# with an `anchor:` commit — nothing absolute, nothing a clone should not
# inherit. Ignoring it meant every clone read `subsystems: 0` and rebuilt a map
# that already existed, and the map's own findings reached nobody. Kept here
# rather than copied to docs/ because crew_state.py:407 hardcodes this path,
# and a second copy is a second thing to drift.
!.crew/codemap/

# ...and the endpoint ledger gizmoduck's security-scan trigger reads
# (crew_state.py's read_endpoints). Same reasoning as the codemap above: a
# scan obligation that only exists in one clone's untracked `.crew/` is not
# an obligation anyone else can see or discharge, so the ledger has to
# travel with the repo like the code it describes does.
!.crew/endpoints.json

# ...and the verification map. This one is a PRACTICE CHANGE, made 2026-09-14,
# not a restatement of what was already true: `verify.json` was ignored like
# everything else here, and README/commands/review.md said so. But the map is
# the per-path rule set - which checks run on which files, which specialist
# reviews which change - and that is a fact about the CODE, decided once, not a
# fact about this machine. Ignored, every clone read "no verification map" and
# selected no specialist, which is indistinguishable from "no rule matched".
# Clone-safety was claimed here on the strength of a regex sweep for `C:\` and
# `/Users/`. That sweep did not look for the Git Bash `/c/...` form, and the map
# contained exactly that: a rule invoking
# "/c/Program Files/PowerShell/7/pwsh" absolutely, which verify-gate.sh executes
# and blocks completion on. A Linux clone with pwsh ON PATH would still have been
# blocked by it. The rule now resolves its interpreter instead, and the withdrawn
# claim is recorded rather than deleted: "checked for absolute paths" was the
# thing that made nobody look again. What IS true and re-checkable: the file
# carries no URL, hostname or credential, and its top-level keys are `_note`,
# `anchor`, `default`, `rules`, `unmapped`, `version`. Any `run` command added
# here has to resolve its tools, because this file travels now.
# The machine-specific half of crew's state is `config.json` (obsidian.vaultPath,
# boardDir, pm.authority), and that one stays ignored above.
!.crew/verify.json

# Written by the GitHub Copilot CLI when --model is passed; pins the model for
# anyone running `copilot` in this repo. crew owns provider model choice
# (qa.copilot.model / dev.copilot.model), so this file must not shadow it.
.github/copilot/

# graphify code graph. graph.json is deliberately NOT ignored: .gitattributes
# registers a union merge driver for it, and a union driver only has something
# to merge if both sides are tracked. Everything else here is rebuildable —
# cache/ alone is 4.6 MB and churns on every commit via the post-commit hook.
# Allowlist, not a denylist: a build drops cache/, .graphify_analysis.json,
# .graphify_labels.json(.sig), .graphify_root, GRAPH_REPORT.md, manifest.json
# and a 2.6 MB graph.html, and the set grows with the tool. Enumerating them
# means the next new artifact lands untracked-but-visible and gets committed by
# accident. Ignore the directory and re-admit the one file that must be tracked.
graphify-out/*
!graphify-out/graph.json

# The second re-admission, and deliberately only the second. GRAPH_REPORT.md is
# the human-readable half of the map — community hubs, freshness, the
# EXTRACTED/INFERRED split — and it is what someone reads to orient in a repo
# they did not build. graph.json is 7 MB of machine-readable nodes; nobody
# reads it. 132 KB of markdown that regenerates on every commit is real diff
# noise, and that is the price: without it the only map that travels is one no
# human opens. graph.html stays ignored — 382 KB of generated viewer, not a map.
!graphify-out/GRAPH_REPORT.md

# --- crew local state ------------------------------------------------------
# Raw transcripts contain everything the session saw, including any secret
# that reached it. Never commit them.
.crew/transcripts/
.crew/state.json
.work/*.local.md

# --- crew promotion gate ---------------------------------------------------
# promote-gate.sh refuses to deploy when the tree is dirty, and separately
# requires an approval marker at .crew/.approved-<env>-<sha>. The OPERATOR
# creates that marker; the gate only looks for it (promote-gate.sh:235). The
# file the gate writes itself is .crew/.deploy-in-flight (promote-gate.sh:288).
# An earlier version of this comment said the gate writes the approval marker,
# which made the deadlock sound automatic when it is not.
#
# This block used to assert that ".crew/ and .work/ are ALREADY fully ignored
# above" and that "the whole of crew's state - config.json, verify.json,
# STATUS.md, INDEX.md, PROMOTIONS.md - is local to each machine and never
# committed". That was false from the moment `!.crew/codemap/` was added ~50
# lines above, and it stayed in the file contradicting its own stanza. The
# policy, stated once: `.crew/*` is ignored, and a NAMED list is un-ignored -
# `!.crew/codemap/`, `!.crew/endpoints.json`, `!.crew/verify.json`. Everything
# else under `.crew/`, including `config.json` and `STATUS.md`, is machine-local.
# `.work/` is ignored entirely, PROMOTIONS.md included.
#
# These two entries ARE redundant belt-and-braces, and saying otherwise was the
# second wrong claim in this block. `.crew/*` already ignores both, and none of
# the three negations above re-admits either - `git check-ignore` confirms
# `.crew/.approved-production-abc` and `.crew/x.lock` are ignored with these two
# lines deleted. They are kept because they NAME the files whose tracking would
# wedge a promotion, which is documentation a future narrowing of the base
# ignore would need; they are not what makes the deadlock unreachable today.
# Their position below the negations is likewise harmless rather than
# load-bearing. `check_crew_ignore_policy` now proves all of this by asking git
# instead of reading these lines. Every clone still starts with no crew config
# and must run /crew:init itself.
.crew/.approved-*
.crew/*.lock
.work/cache/

# --- localgpu ------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-repo config carries absolute, machine-specific paths; the index itself
# lives under %LOCALAPPDATA%\localgpu and never enters the tree.
.localgpu/

# Rendered diagram output. `render.sh` regenerates every PNG/SVG from the
# tracked `.mmd` sources beside them, and a binary that changes on every
# re-render is pure diff churn - the same reason graphify-out/ is ignored
# except for the one file that must travel. The .mmd IS the source of truth
# and is tracked; anyone who wants the pictures runs:
#   bash <crew>/skills/crew-diagrams/scripts/render.sh docs/diagrams
docs/diagrams/out/

# Project-scope MCP registration. Written by `/localgpu:setup` with LITERAL
# absolute paths - the venv interpreter, the plugin cache directory (version-
# pinned), and LOCALGPU_HOME - because Claude Code reads this file directly and
# does not expand `~` or a variable. Every one of those is machine-specific, so
# a committed copy spawns a server pointing at paths that exist on exactly one
# box. Same reasoning as `.localgpu/` two lines up.
#
# It also pins the plugin version: bump localgpu and this file points at a
# cache directory that no longer exists, and the server fails to spawn with no
# useful error. Re-run `/localgpu:setup` after a localgpu upgrade.
.mcp.json

# docs/security-scans/ is TRACKED, and that is a decision, not an oversight.
# It was previously committed only by omission - no entry here, so the default
# applied and nobody had chosen. Recorded so it is not later "fixed" by someone
# assuming the absence was an accident.
#
# The tradeoff was weighed and taken deliberately: a scan report names
# hostnames, live endpoints and unpatched findings, and committing it puts that
# in git history where it survives deletion and travels to every clone and
# fork. Against that, the evidence a promote gate checks has to exist on a
# fresh checkout - `crew_state._artifact_confirms_scan` reads the artifact and
# requires gizmoduck's own `**Total finding instances:**` marker, so an ignored
# report means CI sees every endpoint as permanently unscanned.
#
# Tracked wins. If that trade is ever revisited, the alternative is to ignore
# the reports and track a minimal receipt per endpoint (id, timestamp,
# tool+version, counts by severity) and repoint `_artifact_confirms_scan` at
# the receipt - which is real work with its own tests, not a one-line flip.

# The sabotage harness backs a source file up as `<file>.bak` before it
# mutates it, and `<file>.bak.partial` while that backup is being written.
# `7c420e90` swept `crew_state.py.bak` into a commit via `git add -A`, and a
# TRACKED backup is the damaging case: `plugin/crew/tests/sabotage.py` refuses
# to start when one is present, so a committed `.bak` gates the suite off
# permanently on every clone.
#
# Ignoring it is what stops that happening again. It does NOT preserve any
# `git status` signal -- ignored files are hidden from `git status`, so this
# trades one diagnostic for another and the trade is worth naming: the harness
# checks the FILESYSTEM at startup, which is a check that runs whether or not
# anybody looks at `git status`. Use `git status --ignored` to see one by eye.
*.bak
*.bak.partial

# Generated reports. `report-builder` writes here, and these documents name
# accounts, hosts and weak configuration - they read as an attack plan and must
# never reach the remote. Ignored wholesale rather than per-file, for the same
# reason skills/power-automate-api/ ignores its snapshot directory: one such
# file already reached this public repo, and it was a review that caught it
# rather than a person reading the diff.
reports/

# `claude plugin eval` results. Written fresh by every run of
# scripts/run-plugin-evals.{sh,ps1} (or a bare `claude plugin eval` invocation)
# under <eval dir>/results/<timestamp>/ - aggregate-result.json and a
# self-contained report.html per run, per
# https://code.claude.com/docs/en/plugin-evals#read-the-results, which says
# outright to add this to .gitignore. Committing one would freeze a transcript
# of a real model conversation (an agent's tool calls and replies) as of one
# run, going stale the moment the plugin or the model changes.
plugin/crew/evals/results/
